1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,259
Take a square sheet of tin, eighteen centimetres a side.

2
00:00:04,259 --> 00:00:09,338
Cut a small square from each corner and fold up the flaps: an open box.

3
00:00:09,338 --> 00:00:12,033
Cut too little and the box is shallow.

4
00:00:12,033 --> 00:00:14,282
Cut too much and the base vanishes.

5
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:18,226
Somewhere in between is the biggest possible box.

6
00:00:18,226 --> 00:00:21,116
At the top of the curve the slope is zero.

7
00:00:21,116 --> 00:00:28,942
Derivatives find it exactly: cut three centimetres, and the box holds four hundred and thirty two cubic centimetres.

8
00:00:28,942 --> 00:00:35,607
This unit is about using derivatives: for slopes, rates, approximations and the best possible answer.

9
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:45,540
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 12: Derivatives.

10
00:00:47,933 --> 00:00:49,119
Seven parts.

11
00:00:49,119 --> 00:00:51,906
Hyperbolic functions and their derivatives.

12
00:00:51,906 --> 00:00:54,940
L'Hospital's rule for awkward limits.

13
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:57,466
Differentials and small errors.

14
00:00:57,466 --> 00:00:59,106
Tangents and normals.

15
00:00:59,106 --> 00:01:00,396
Rates of change.

16
00:01:00,396 --> 00:01:03,820
Increasing functions, maxima and minima.

17
00:01:03,820 --> 00:01:04,874
And concavity.

18
00:01:09,233 --> 00:01:12,034
Cos and sin trace the unit circle.

19
00:01:12,034 --> 00:01:24,196
Their cousins, the hyperbolic cosine and sine, built from e to the x and e to the minus x, trace the unit hyperbola, x squared minus y squared equals one.

20
00:01:24,196 --> 00:01:27,604
So cosh squared minus sinh squared is always one.

21
00:01:27,833 --> 00:01:38,824
Differentiate straight from the exponential definitions: the derivative of sinh is cosh, and of cosh is sinh, with no minus sign at all, unlike cos.

22
00:01:38,824 --> 00:01:41,565
And tanh differentiates to sech squared.

23
00:01:42,767 --> 00:01:45,217
And the inverse hyperbolic functions.

24
00:01:45,217 --> 00:01:58,146
Inverse sinh differentiates to one over root of x squared plus one; inverse cosh to one over root of x squared minus one; inverse tanh to one over one minus x squared.

25
00:01:58,146 --> 00:02:02,917
Compare them with the inverse trig derivatives: the signs inside swap.

26
00:02:04,133 --> 00:02:04,843
Four quick ones.

27
00:02:05,367 --> 00:02:10,095
Chain rule for the first: two x sinh of x squared plus one.

28
00:02:10,095 --> 00:02:13,606
The second is one over cosh times sinh: tanh x.

29
00:02:13,833 --> 00:02:18,521
Product rule: cosh squared plus sinh squared, which is cosh two x.

30
00:02:18,733 --> 00:02:25,117
And the inverse sinh of x over three tidies up to one over root of x squared plus nine.

31
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:37,264
A limit like sin two x over x is nought over nought at zero: useless as it stands.

32
00:02:37,264 --> 00:02:39,504
But look at the graphs near zero.

33
00:02:39,504 --> 00:02:44,933
Both pass through the origin, and close in, each looks like its tangent line.

34
00:02:44,933 --> 00:02:48,302
So their ratio approaches the ratio of their slopes.

35
00:02:48,533 --> 00:02:50,201
That is L'Hospital's rule.

36
00:02:50,201 --> 00:03:00,527
For a nought over nought, or infinity over infinity form, differentiate the top and the bottom separately, not as a quotient, and take the limit again.

37
00:03:00,527 --> 00:03:05,281
Sin two x over x becomes two cos two x over one: two.

38
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:08,133
Sometimes once is not enough.

39
00:03:08,333 --> 00:03:10,769
Check the form: nought over nought.

40
00:03:10,769 --> 00:03:12,918
Differentiate top and bottom.

41
00:03:12,918 --> 00:03:14,403
Still nought over nought.

42
00:03:14,633 --> 00:03:16,589
Again: still nought over nought.

43
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:22,088
A third time: e to the zero plus e to the zero, over cos zero.

44
00:03:22,088 --> 00:03:22,433
Two.

45
00:03:24,433 --> 00:03:26,072
Other indeterminate forms.

46
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:31,119
Infinity over infinity: twice, and the exponential wins.

47
00:03:31,119 --> 00:03:31,603
Zero.

48
00:03:31,833 --> 00:03:38,241
Nought times infinity is not a quotient, so rewrite it as log x over one over x.

49
00:03:38,241 --> 00:03:41,562
Then the rule gives minus x, which goes to zero.

50
00:03:41,767 --> 00:03:44,203
Nought to the nought: take logs.

51
00:03:44,203 --> 00:03:49,521
Log y is x log x, which we just found tends to zero, so y tends to one.

52
00:03:51,533 --> 00:03:52,719
Two warnings.

53
00:03:52,719 --> 00:03:57,498
It is not the quotient rule: differentiate top and bottom separately.

54
00:03:57,498 --> 00:04:02,233
And check the form before every step; once it stops being nought over nought, stop.

55
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,713
Near a point, a curve and its tangent almost agree.

56
00:04:10,713 --> 00:04:13,473
Step dx along the x axis.

57
00:04:13,473 --> 00:04:17,705
The true change in y, along the curve, is delta y.

58
00:04:17,705 --> 00:04:24,150
The change along the tangent, f dash of x times dx, is the differential dy.

59
00:04:24,150 --> 00:04:26,288
The red gap between them is the error.

60
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:31,723
Shrink the step and the red gap vanishes much faster than the step itself.

61
00:04:31,723 --> 00:04:40,268
So for small changes, f of x plus delta x is approximately f of x plus f dash of x times delta x.

62
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:43,644
Pick a nearby perfect square.

63
00:04:43,867 --> 00:04:52,481
Root forty nine is seven; the derivative there is one over fourteen; times a half: point zero three five seven.

64
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:59,844
So root forty nine point five is about seven point zero three five seven: right to four places.

65
00:05:01,867 --> 00:05:02,381
Errors.

66
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:09,330
The volume error is four pi r squared times d r: about twelve point three cubic centimetres.

67
00:05:09,533 --> 00:05:15,938
Divide by V: the relative error in a cube of anything is three times the relative error in the length.

68
00:05:16,167 --> 00:05:21,340
So a point one per cent error in a cube's side gives a point three per cent error in its volume.

69
00:05:26,933 --> 00:05:30,646
The derivative at a point is the slope of the tangent there.

70
00:05:30,646 --> 00:05:41,931
The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so its slope is minus one over m. Slide along y equals x cubed minus three x plus two and watch the slope change.

71
00:05:43,133 --> 00:05:45,183
Exactly the curve we just explored.

72
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:48,150
At x equals two the slope is nine.

73
00:05:48,367 --> 00:05:51,750
Tangent: y equals nine x minus fourteen.

74
00:05:51,967 --> 00:05:57,829
Normal: slope minus a ninth, so x plus nine y minus thirty eight equals zero.

75
00:05:59,833 --> 00:06:03,553
The angle between two curves is the angle between their tangents.

76
00:06:03,767 --> 00:06:09,235
Slopes at one, one: two, and, by implicit differentiation, a half.

77
00:06:09,467 --> 00:06:16,083
Tan theta is m one minus m two over one plus their product: three quarters.

78
00:06:16,083 --> 00:06:18,664
Thirty six degrees fifty two minutes.

79
00:06:24,233 --> 00:06:27,438
Rates of change are derivatives with respect to time.

80
00:06:27,438 --> 00:06:33,492
A five metre ladder leans on a wall, and its foot is pulled away at two metres per second.

81
00:06:33,492 --> 00:06:40,157
The foot moves steadily, but watch the top: it starts slowly, then plunges faster and faster.

82
00:06:40,367 --> 00:06:46,606
The link between x and y is Pythagoras: x squared plus y squared is twenty five.

83
00:06:46,606 --> 00:06:48,806
Differentiate with respect to time.

84
00:06:48,806 --> 00:06:56,410
When the foot is four metres out, the top is three metres up, and it is falling at eight thirds metres per second.

85
00:06:56,410 --> 00:06:58,940
The minus sign means y is decreasing.

86
00:07:00,167 --> 00:07:07,747
Water pours at a steady three cubic metres a minute into a cone ten metres deep with a five metre radius.

87
00:07:07,747 --> 00:07:12,195
The level rises fast at first and slows as the cone widens.

88
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:17,506
Similar triangles give r equals h over two, which removes r.

89
00:07:17,506 --> 00:07:20,930
So V is pi h cubed over twelve.

90
00:07:20,930 --> 00:07:31,503
Differentiate with respect to time, put in three and h equals four: the level is rising at three over four pi, about a quarter of a metre a minute.

91
00:07:32,733 --> 00:07:33,385
One more rate.

92
00:07:33,967 --> 00:07:36,577
Differentiate the volume with respect to time.

93
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:43,076
Substitute: one over nine pi, about point zero three five centimetres per second.

94
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:47,108
Exam tip.

95
00:07:47,108 --> 00:07:54,365
Draw it, link the quantities by one equation, differentiate with respect to time, and only then put in the numbers.

96
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:04,418
Where the derivative is positive the graph climbs; where it is negative the graph falls.

97
00:08:04,418 --> 00:08:16,076
Here is f of x equals two x cubed minus nine x squared plus twelve x plus five, and below it, its derivative, six times x minus one times x minus two.

98
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:17,291
Walk along.

99
00:08:17,291 --> 00:08:22,408
Before x equals one, f dash is positive: rising.

100
00:08:22,408 --> 00:08:25,455
Between one and two, negative: falling.

101
00:08:25,455 --> 00:08:27,604
After two, positive again.

102
00:08:27,604 --> 00:08:34,431
At one and two the derivative is zero: the graph turns, at a local maximum and a local minimum.

103
00:08:34,633 --> 00:08:38,020
To tell which is which: the second derivative.

104
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:41,731
Negative means the curve bends down, so a maximum.

105
00:08:41,731 --> 00:08:44,218
Positive bends up: a minimum.

106
00:08:44,218 --> 00:08:49,000
Zero: the test fails, and you must check whether f dash changes sign.

107
00:08:50,233 --> 00:08:51,278
The standard routine.

108
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:57,358
Stationary points at one and three; the second derivative is six x minus twelve.

109
00:08:57,567 --> 00:09:00,892
At one it is negative: a maximum, nineteen.

110
00:09:01,100 --> 00:09:03,999
At three positive: a minimum, fifteen.

111
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:07,281
The cold open's box.

112
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:10,999
The base is eighteen minus two x, the height x.

113
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:17,041
Differentiate and factorise: x is three or nine, and nine leaves no base at all.

114
00:09:17,267 --> 00:09:23,730
The second derivative is negative at three: a maximum of four hundred and thirty two cubic centimetres.

115
00:09:25,733 --> 00:09:26,842
On a closed interval.

116
00:09:27,067 --> 00:09:30,387
One stationary point inside: x equals two.

117
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:33,282
Now evaluate at it and at both endpoints.

118
00:09:33,500 --> 00:09:38,970
The biggest is eighty nine, at an endpoint where the derivative is not zero.

119
00:09:38,970 --> 00:09:41,744
That is why endpoints must always be checked.

120
00:09:47,333 --> 00:09:51,033
The second derivative tells you how the slope is changing.

121
00:09:51,033 --> 00:09:58,533
Where it is positive, the tangent turns anticlockwise and the curve bends upward, like a cup: concave up.

122
00:09:58,533 --> 00:10:01,202
Where negative, it bends down, like a cap.

123
00:10:01,202 --> 00:10:06,511
Ride a tangent along x to the fourth minus four x cubed plus ten.

124
00:10:06,733 --> 00:10:12,985
The second derivative, twelve x times x minus two, changes sign at zero and at two.

125
00:10:12,985 --> 00:10:16,930
Those are the points of inflection, where the bending switches.

126
00:10:16,930 --> 00:10:23,896
Notice x equals zero: the slope is zero there too, but it is not a maximum or minimum.

127
00:10:23,896 --> 00:10:27,711
The only extreme is the minimum at x equals three.

128
00:10:30,133 --> 00:10:31,437
Pause and think.

129
00:10:31,437 --> 00:10:32,400
Use L'Hospital.

130
00:10:39,300 --> 00:10:44,536
Three: differentiate top and bottom to get three cos three x over one.

131
00:10:46,533 --> 00:10:46,968
One more.

132
00:10:54,600 --> 00:10:58,377
A local maximum: the curve is bending downward at a flat point.

133
00:11:00,567 --> 00:11:01,662
Quick recap.

134
00:11:01,662 --> 00:11:03,655
The hyperbolic derivatives.

135
00:11:03,655 --> 00:11:06,662
L'Hospital's rule for nought over nought.

136
00:11:06,662 --> 00:11:09,136
Differentials for small errors.

137
00:11:09,136 --> 00:11:12,157
Tangents, normals and related rates.

138
00:11:12,157 --> 00:11:18,120
And the first and second derivatives for rising, falling, maxima, minima and bending.

139
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:23,708
That was derivatives.

140
00:11:23,708 --> 00:11:29,046
Next, anti-derivatives: running differentiation backwards to find integrals.

141
00:11:29,046 --> 00:11:29,631
See you there.

